    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking for some help with my Monstera deliciosa. It's been sitting in my living room for a year and doing great, but lately, the lower leaves are starting to turn a pale yellow color. The soil feels kind of damp even when I think I've watered it, but I don't see any obvious pests. Any idea what might be going on?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There could be a few reasons for your Monstera's yellowing lower leaves. Overwatering, as you suspect, is a common culprit, especially if the soil is consistently damp. It's possible that the plant is experiencing root rot, which can occur when excess moisture causes the roots to decay. Check the roots by gently removing the plant from its pot to see if they're showing any signs of rot or decay.",
      "source": "target",
